WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.540
In the late 1960s, a young engineer at JPL named Gary Flandro was running the math on how planetary positions repeat.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:18.103
He noticed that in the late 1970s, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune would all sit on the same side of the Sun, roughly in line.

00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:27.665
A single spacecraft could use Jupiter's gravity to slingshot to Saturn, Saturn's to slingshot to Uranus, Uranus's to reach Neptune.

00:00:27.665 --> 00:00:32.993
The alignment comes around once every one hundred and seventy-five years.

00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:37.592
The previous one had been during Thomas Jefferson's presidency.

00:00:37.592 --> 00:00:41.242
The next one will be in the twenty-second century.

00:00:41.242 --> 00:00:45.110
Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 launched in 1977 to catch it.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:51.607
Voyager 2 flew past Jupiter in 1979, Saturn in 1981, Uranus in 1986, and Neptune in 1989.

00:00:51.607 --> 00:00:56.497
It is the only spacecraft to have visited Uranus or Neptune at all.

00:00:56.497 --> 00:01:08.176
Voyager 1 took a different option at Saturn — it traded Uranus and Neptune for a close look at Titan's atmosphere, then turned upward out of the ecliptic plane.

00:01:08.176 --> 00:01:10.804
Both spacecraft are still operating.

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:18.834
Voyager 1 is the most distant human-made object — currently about twenty-four billion kilometers from the Sun.

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:21.972
It crossed into interstellar space in 2012.

00:01:21.972 --> 00:01:27.593
Voyager 2 followed in 2018, on a different trajectory through the heliopause.

00:01:27.593 --> 00:01:39.929
Each spacecraft carries the Golden Record — a phonograph disc with recordings of natural sounds, music from twenty-seven cultures, and greetings in fifty-five languages.

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.797
Carl Sagan led the team that chose what to put on it.

00:01:43.797 --> 00:01:53.360
The cover has instructions, in pulsar-position notation and hydrogen-atom diagrams, for any future intelligence that might find it.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.885
The spacecraft will keep going for tens of thousands of years.

00:01:57.885 --> 00:02:07.666
They will outlive the agencies that built them, the countries that funded them, and possibly the species that listened to the records.

00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:14.455
Most of the missions you will see in this catalogue were possible because Voyager went first.

